      \field{abstract}{This study explores a machine learning-based approach for predicting the micro-thrust generated by laser ablation of aluminum alloy materials using laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy (LIBS). An impulse-spectroscopy coupled measurement technique is introduced to construct datasets combining plasma spectral information and impulse coupling coefficients under various laser parameters. Using these datasets, support vector machine, and Neural Network models are developed to establish relationships between plasma spectral parameters and the propulsion performance of high-energy pulsed laser ablation. The models demonstrate the ability to predict impulse coupling coefficients based on plasma spectral data, achieving an R2 value of up to 0.998 for aluminum alloy targets under specific conditions. These findings highlight the potential of LIBS-based methods for measuring laser propulsion performance and provide data-driven insights for understanding the interactions between laser ablation and material properties.}
