      \field{abstract}{Reliability-based design optimization (RBDO) is widely applied to address uncertainty in structural design. However, the traditional surrogate model approaches train independent models for each probabilistic constraint, thus neglecting underlying (potentially nonlinear) interactions among these constraints. This study incorporates a multi-task learning mechanism into the safe region during the training phase of the support vector regression (SVR) surrogate model. Thus, an effective multi-constraint SVR surrogate model is produced. The multi-constraint SVR surrogate model serves as an effective surrogate for multiple probabilistic constraint functions and integrates a multi-task learning mechanism to capture interactions among these constraints. In the RBDO framework, the objective function and multi-constraint formulations are systematically established through the proposed multi-constraint SVR surrogate model, with the pelican optimization algorithm subsequently executing the solution search process. This biologically-inspired algorithm, derived from observations of pelicans' coordinated foraging behaviors, demonstrates superior effectiveness in overcoming RBDO's inherent computational challenges. This proposed RBDO framework is validated through four case studies involving complex probabilistic constraints. Results indicate significant improvement in computational efficiency while maintaining high accuracy. Moreover, iterative convergence curves and design parameter collections from case studies support the development of high-reliability, high-performance RBDO strategies.}
